{
  "id": "r02",
  "text": "You wake up on the floor. There is a door ahead of you, and beyond it, a corridor that looks almost disappointingly ordinary. A light fitting, family photographs, a cabinet, a radio. The game gives you very little distance to travel, so you start learning the details without trying. Then you reach the end, go through the door, and arrive in that corridor again. The important part is what changes on the next pass. A familiar picture has become a clue. You start wondering what else you missed. The light changes, and the same corner suddenly feels like somewhere you shouldn't approach. Because the geography is familiar, a small difference gets your full attention. You can't dismiss it as another room you haven't explored yet. P.T. keeps asking you to look more closely at the place you already know. That creates a nasty little negotiation: you want to make progress, but progress requires approaching the thing that has just made you uncomfortable. Watch the camera pause at the closed door, then check the room behind it. The player has to decide where to look next. A film can choose where your attention goes; this makes that nervous inspection your responsibility.",
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
